<article class="env-proposition" id="719D">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/719D">Proposition <span data-tag="719D">1.2.12.9</span> <span class="named">(Joyal)</span>.</a>final object!uniqueness Let $\calC $ be a $\infty $-category, and let $\calC '$ be the full subcategory of $\calC $ spanned by the final vertices of $\calC $. Then $\calC '$ is either empty or a contractible Kan complex. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We wish to prove that every map $p: \bd \Delta ^ n \rightarrow \calC '$ can be extended to an $n$-simplex of $\calC '$. If $n = 0$, this is possible unless $\calC '$ is empty. For $n > 0$, the desired extension exists because $p$ carries the $n$th vertex of $\bd \Delta ^ n$ to a final object of $\calC $. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>